\description{
This function computes the conditional AIC (cAIC) of the isoscape models
fitted with \code{\link{isofit}} or \code{\link{isomultifit}}, corrected for the number of
parameters estimated in the residual dispersion model. This corrected cAIC
measures the ability of the joint fit of the mean model and of the residual
dispersion model to predict the isotopic values observed at the source
locations. It thus allows for the comparison of isoscape models differing
not only in the structure of their mean model but also in the structure of
their residual dispersion model.
}
\details{
For linear mixed models whose residual errors have a variance (or
covariance) depending on \eqn{r} unknown parameters, Overholser & Xu (2014,
theorem 1 and equation 6) showed that the plug-in estimate of the cAIC is
\eqn{-2 \log L_c + 2 (\textrm{tr}(H) + r)}{-2 log Lc + 2 (tr(H) + r)}, where
\eqn{\log L_c}{log Lc} is the conditional log-likelihood and
\eqn{\textrm{tr}(H)}{tr(H)} is the effective number of degrees of freedom of
the fixed and random effects (the trace of the "hat" matrix). The cAIC
computed by \pkg{spaMM} for the mean model (\code{mean_fit}) is
\eqn{-2 \log L_c + 2 \textrm{tr}(H)}{-2 log Lc + 2 tr(H)}: it treats the
residual variances (\code{pred_disp}) as known, while they have been predicted
from the fit of the residual dispersion model (\code{disp_fit}). Following
Courtiol & Rousset (2017), we thus add the missing term by considering the
parameters estimated in the residual dispersion model (\code{K_disp}) as the
parameters of the residual variance of the mean model (i.e. \eqn{r = K_{disp}}{r = K_disp}):

\deqn{cAIC = cAIC_{mean\_fit} + 2 \times K_{disp}}

\code{K_disp} is the number of fixed-effect coefficients, plus the number of
random-effect variances, plus the number of parameters of the Matérn
correlation function (including the nugget, if any), plus the number of
residual dispersion parameters that have been estimated in \code{disp_fit} (see
\code{\link[spaMM:DoF]{spaMM::DoF}}). Parameters kept fixed during the fit (e.g. the residual
variance of \code{disp_fit}, which is fixed to its theoretical expectation) are not
counted.

For a fit produced by \code{\link{isomultifit}}, the cAIC is the sum of the corrected
cAIC of all the pairs of models (one per strata), since all pairs are fitted
independently on non-overlapping data. Similarly, \code{K_mean} and \code{K_disp} are
summed over all pairs.

As for any information criterion, only models fitted on the same data can be
compared. The lower the cAIC, the better the predictive power of the fitted
isoscape. Since the goal of an isoscape is prediction, there is no need to
favour simpler models beyond what the cAIC already accounts for. Note,
however, that the cAIC only reflects the quality of the predictions at the
source locations, so always check the isoscapes produced by the selected
model (e.g. by plotting the prediction variance; see \code{\link{isoscape}}).

This correction should be seen as an approximation. First, the result of
Overholser & Xu (2014) is asymptotic and the plug-in cAIC can be biased in
small samples (they also discuss alternative corrections for small samples).
Second, their result assumes that the parameters of the residual variance
are estimated jointly with the other parameters of the model, whereas
\pkg{IsoriX} estimates them in a separate fit (\code{disp_fit}). Third, the
residual variances predicted by \code{disp_fit} also depend on the predicted
values of its random effects, which are not counted in \code{K_disp}. Finally,
the cAIC has been shown to tend to favour models with additional random
effects (Greven & Kneib 2010; Overholser & Xu 2014), so comparisons of models
differing in their random-effect structure should be interpreted with care.
}
